Hello, and welcome to TechScape. I=E2=80=99m Blake Montgomery, US tech edit=
or at the Guardian. Today in tech, we=E2=80=99re discussing the past week=
=E2=80=99s all-consuming apoplexy over AI safety.

Why did this warning about the AI apocalypse break through when so many oth=
ers didn=E2=80=99t?

Less than a week ago, a young Anthropic researcher announced his resignatio=
n from the AI giant on X, in a post that has shaken Silicon Valley, Washing=
ton DC, and even Beijing.

=E2=80=9CThe people building AI earnestly believe that it could kill us all=
 by the end of the decade,=E2=80=9D wrote Jacob Coxon.

Since then, the post has been viewed 171m times, per X=E2=80=99s metrics. W=
hy did it travel so far?

It=E2=80=99s not the first time AI insiders have issued dire warnings. Ever=
y few months for the past several years, an AI researcher has resigned in a=
 moment of crisis, proclaiming the impending apocalypse. His proclamation c=
omes at a unique political confluence, a moment of high-strung public fear =
and rage.

Loudly declaring that your peers in Silicon Valley are failing at AI safety=
 is as old as the companies themselves. OpenAI=E2=80=99s board fired Sam Al=
tman over safety concerns in 2023. Dario Amodei split from OpenAI five year=
s ago with the aim of founding a =E2=80=9Csafety-first=E2=80=9D lab. In 201=
5, Altman and Elon Musk founded OpenAI as a counterweight to Google=E2=80=
=99s approach to AI. Musk sued Altman for failing to keep a fundamental saf=
ety promise and lost.

Major AI researchers have made second careers of predicting doom. Geoffrey =
Hinton, a Nobel laureate known as =E2=80=9Cthe godfather of AI=E2=80=9D, sa=
id as far back as 2023 that the technology could cause human extinction wit=
hin three decades. He=E2=80=99s since shortened his timeline. He resigned f=
rom Google for reasons similar to those of Coxon. Hinton has been on the sp=
eaking circuit ever since, prophesying doom.

Hinton=E2=80=99s resignation even inspired a significant response from lawm=
akers. In the US, Joe Biden issued an executive order mandating safety test=
ing by AI companies. Congress summoned AI CEOs for a grilling, though that =
resulted in little legislative action. In the European Union, the EU Artifi=
cal Intelligence Act passed in part due to a strong reaction to Hinton=E2=
=80=99s warnings.

And yet, here we are again, burdened with the feeling that little meaningfu=
l regulation is preventing harm by AI.

So why is Coxon=E2=80=99s apocalyptic proclamation percolating deeper?

First, there=E2=80=99s the messenger: Coxon=E2=80=99s call for greater safe=
ty is credible by several metrics: He had insider access to both Anthropic =
and OpenAI, where he worked for five years. He resigned before he would hav=
e received equity in Anthropic, demonstrating the personal cost of speaking=
 out. And his colleagues, still on Anthropic=E2=80=99s payroll, corroborate=
d his assertion that many AI researchers believe there=E2=80=99s a possibil=
ity that AI will bring about human extinction, with one co-worker gauging t=
hat outcome as more than 10% likely.

Recent incidents in the AI field have made apocalyptic claims more credible=
. A swarm of AI agents operated by OpenAI went rogue, broke containment and=
 hacked into multiple sites and organizations during safety testing with st=
artling coordination earlier this year. On Thursday, Anthropic detailed how=
 criminals and rogue scientists had attempted to use Claude to make bioweap=
ons.

=E2=80=9CThe reason this time is different is that out-of-control AI is not=
 in the future, but in the past. People have seen evidence of it happening,=
=E2=80=9D argued Max Tegmark, co-founder of the Future of Life Institute, w=
hich has been funded with hundreds of millions of dollars from the billiona=
ire founder of the cryptocurrency Ethereum. (Tegmark=E2=80=99s organization=
 already in 2023 called for a slowdown of AI development, which at the time=
 provoked a rejection from Amodei.)

=E2=80=9CIf you had tried to get people worried about nuclear winter in 194=
2, good luck. People would ask whether a nuclear weapon was even possible,=
=E2=80=9D said Tegmark. =E2=80=9CIt was obvious to physicists then, but you=
 couldn=E2=80=99t have freaked out normal people. It was all theoretical. W=
ith AI, it was all theoretical until this happened. Now the debate over whe=
ther you can lose control is over.=E2=80=9D

Then there=E2=80=99s the context. The general feeling towards technology in=
 the US is kindling-sensitive, and claims of impending doom are fiery. The =
country is in the throes of a multifaceted backlash to technology. People o=
utside major tech hubs feel imposed upon physically by data centers and fig=
uratively by AI=E2=80=99s takeover of the workplace.

Looming over the country are tech billionaires =E2=80=93 in control of vast=
 wealth, global communication channels =E2=80=93 throwing their weight arou=
nd in US politics with
