.

=E2=80=9CIt=E2=80=99s been at least over a year of steadily increasing data=
 center pushback that has made being pro-AI regulation the new common sense=
, and it certainly has made being pro-AI industry a very toxic brand,=E2=80=
=9D said Amba Kak, co-executive director of the AI Now Institute and a form=
er senior adviser on AI for the US Federal Trade Commission.

There=E2=80=99s the fact that the companies involved have responded vigorou=
sly to Coxon. Their pledged commitments have details and heft.

=E2=80=9CIt allowed for companies like Anthropic and others to position the=
mselves immediately as the primary experts in how to deal with these fears.=
 How to solve problems that, by every account, they have created or are bes=
t positioned to prevent,=E2=80=9D Kak said.

Two closing thoughts. In a sign of how broad the crisis of confidence in AI=
 has become: Strange bedfellows have partnered up in response. The Future o=
f Life Institute is hosting a rally today in Washington promoting AI safety=
 with headliners Bernie Sanders, the progressive senator from Vermont, and =
Steve Bannon, former chief of staff to Donald Trump, who have been sworn en=
emies on other issues.

And finally: Why is the AI industry so full of men =E2=80=93 only men in AI=
 have expressed these fears of human extinction =E2=80=93 in the throes of =
a crisis over the extinctive potential of their work?

=E2=80=9CThese people certainly believe what they=E2=80=99re saying. It=E2=
=80=99s not a fringe view,=E2=80=9D said Shakeel Hashim, writer of the
